WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.780 --> 00:00:03.823
Jamie Yancovitz: The
actors are not properly

00:00:03.823 --> 00:00:05.450
chambering their weapons.

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:07.786
When you have the sticks,
every time you strike,

00:00:07.786 --> 00:00:10.330
you chamber. You
strike, you chamber.

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:12.373
My name is Jamie Yancovitz.

00:00:12.373 --> 00:00:14.793
I'm the creator of Survival Arts Academy.

00:00:14.793 --> 00:00:18.129
We use the indigenous fighting
arts of the Philippines,

00:00:18.129 --> 00:00:21.633
also known as Kali, Arnis, or Eskrima.

00:00:21.633 --> 00:00:23.426
Today, we're going to
look at some of the movies

00:00:23.426 --> 00:00:25.720
that have popularized
Filipino fighting arts,

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:27.305
some of the weapons that are used,

00:00:27.305 --> 00:00:29.891
and how realistic the
fight scenes really are.

00:00:37.649 --> 00:00:39.984
He has to be very careful
in this situation.

00:00:39.984 --> 00:00:41.861
Even though they're 
in a public setting,

00:00:41.861 --> 00:00:44.280
this is a
close-quarters-combat fight.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:46.741
This is them getting very,
very close to each other

00:00:46.741 --> 00:00:49.994
and him not having a lot of
time to respond to the blade.

00:00:50.995 --> 00:00:53.790
The Winter Soldier throwing
the blade and catching it

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:55.333
seems very flashy,

00:00:55.333 --> 00:00:56.876
it doesn't seem to
be quite useful,

00:00:56.876 --> 00:00:58.878
it's not very realistic.

00:00:58.878 --> 00:01:02.048
More seemingly for
the movie effect.

00:01:02.048 --> 00:01:03.967
And then there's a
moment where the blade

00:01:03.967 --> 00:01:06.719
is actually coming at the
Winter Soldier, right there.

00:01:06.719 --> 00:01:07.887
So, in that moment,

00:01:07.887 --> 00:01:11.182
where he's trying to
grab the arm underneath,

00:01:11.182 --> 00:01:14.143
we wouldn't recommend
that kind of response.

00:01:14.143 --> 00:01:15.603
So, the reason being is that

00:01:15.603 --> 00:01:17.856
the hand that's
holding the blade is,

00:01:17.856 --> 00:01:19.774
again, with that
ice pick grip,

00:01:19.774 --> 00:01:22.110
the blade is coming in
right here on the face.

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:23.444
We would encourage students to get

00:01:23.444 --> 00:01:25.822
on this side of the blade, on the outside,

00:01:25.822 --> 00:01:28.366
or try to bring that arm another way.

00:01:31.536 --> 00:01:34.372
It really makes
you think about

00:01:34.372 --> 00:01:37.417
your ability to learn
these kinds of techniques.

00:01:37.417 --> 00:01:40.378
And the truth is that it
really depends on the student.

00:01:40.378 --> 00:01:42.005
It depends on, you know,

00:01:42.005 --> 00:01:44.465
the style of fighting
that you're learning.

00:01:44.465 --> 00:01:48.011
We would have to give "Captain
America" a five out of 10,

00:01:48.011 --> 00:01:50.513
just because it wasn't too realistic.

00:01:54.309 --> 00:01:56.686
As exciting and
great as it is --

00:01:56.686 --> 00:02:00.064
Uma Thurman shows
exceptional reflexes,

00:02:00.064 --> 00:02:01.649
maybe superpower
ones --

00:02:01.649 --> 00:02:03.443
what we would recommend
in survival arts training

00:02:03.443 --> 00:02:05.570
is the way that we step
out of the situation.

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:07.488
We would step 45 degrees,

00:02:07.488 --> 00:02:10.158
and we'd try to be on
the outside of the body

00:02:10.158 --> 00:02:12.577
and the outside of that bladed edge.

00:02:14.537 --> 00:02:17.707
So, we're seeing
Vivica actually stab

00:02:17.707 --> 00:02:21.210
and try to kind of hit
in these broken strikes,

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:25.006
but never actually fully
commits to full, fluid strikes,

00:02:25.006 --> 00:02:27.842
which would make her
movements a lot more realistic

00:02:27.842 --> 00:02:30.345
and believable
and effective

00:02:30.345 --> 00:02:32.931
in trying to kill
the main character.

00:02:32.931 --> 00:02:36.351
The proper way of striking
would be to do fluid strikes,

00:02:36.351 --> 00:02:37.268
whether you're doing it

00:02:37.268 --> 00:02:40.229
with a long blade, a stick, a short blade.

00:02:40.229 --> 00:02:41.856
And so you'll be, you know,

00:02:41.856 --> 00:02:44.317
moving in a more effective means

00:02:44.317 --> 00:02:46.736
and also not wasting any movement.

00:02:49.864 --> 00:02:51.407
So, basically, when
you're holding a knife,

00:02:51.407 --> 00:02:54.285
you're going to be
looking at two main grips.

00:02:54.285 --> 00:02:55.745
So we're going to be looking at

00:02:55.745 --> 00:02:58.498
this very common kind of knife grip,

00:02:58.498 --> 00:03:00.249
which moves in this kind of motion.

00:03:00.249 --> 00:03:01.960
And so you'll see here that Vivica

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:04.921
is actually using this knife grip. OK?

00:03:04.921 --> 00:03:07.090
And then you'll see in comparison that

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:09.926
Uma Thurman is holding
it in this knife grip,

00:03:09.926 --> 00:03:12.553
which is the ice pick
grip. Also very effective.

00:03:12.553 --> 00:03:15.264
So these are two of the best knife grips

00:03:15.264 --> 00:03:16.933
that you want to understand

00:03:16.933 --> 00:03:19.644
and also be able to respond to.

00:03:21.145 --> 00:03:22.814
You know, you can hold it in one hand,

00:03:22.814 --> 00:03:25.525
switch to the other hand, switch back,

00:03:25.525 --> 00:03:27.735
you know, depending on your agility.

00:03:27.735 --> 00:03:30.363
So the idea is that you are never stuck

00:03:30.363 --> 00:03:33.032
with that one movement or that one grip.

00:03:33.032 --> 00:03:34.617
You're always trying
to figure out

00:03:34.617 --> 00:03:37.745
in response to counter
the other person.

00:03:37.745 --> 00:03:40.081
So our critique of Vivica's movements

00:03:40.081 --> 00:03:41.916
are that they're a little bit exaggerated

00:03:41.916 --> 00:03:43.751
and not quite real.

00:03:43.751 --> 00:03:45.420
So we're seeing a lot
of wasted movement here

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:48.339
and a lot of openings for
her to also be attacked.

00:03:48.339 --> 00:03:49.507
For this kind of movie,

00:03:49.507 --> 00:03:52.969
we would have to give a
score of four out of 10,

00:03:52.969 --> 00:03:54.178
but it is a lot of fun,

00:03:54.178 --> 00:03:55.972
and we are big fans of "Kill Bill."

00:04:00.309 --> 00:04:02.311
At first, he has no weapon

00:04:02.311 --> 00:04:04.188
fighting against
multiple weapons

00:04:04.188 --> 00:04:06.232
and somehow manages
to get a weapon.

00:04:06.232 --> 00:04:09.485
So that's great that he was
able to actually disarm,

00:04:09.485 --> 00:04:12.363
and it shows the
different kinds of weapons

00:04:12.363 --> 00:04:13.823
that we actually train in Kali.

00:04:13.823 --> 00:04:16.909
So, you know, we work with
single sticks and double sticks.

00:04:16.909 --> 00:04:20.329
And in this particular film,
they're working with batons.

00:04:20.329 --> 00:04:22.874
A baton is just another
form of stick work.

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:27.920
So, having a baton and
being able to use that

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:30.965
with the fluid strikes
against multiple opponents

00:04:30.965 --> 00:04:32.341
is actually recommended.

00:04:32.341 --> 00:04:34.719
We can trace the roots of these systems

00:04:34.719 --> 00:04:37.972
as early as the 12th
century or the 15th century,

00:04:37.972 --> 00:04:40.641
when the Spanish
colonized the Philippines.

00:04:40.641 --> 00:04:43.603
And a lot of systems
are even traced to 1898,

00:04:43.603 --> 00:04:45.521
with the American colonization period.

00:04:45.521 --> 00:04:48.483
During these times,
weapons were confiscated

00:04:48.483 --> 00:04:51.235
and banned by the colonial forces,

00:04:51.235 --> 00:04:55.281
so indigenous peoples creatively hid

00:04:55.281 --> 00:04:56.616
in different ways.

00:04:56.616 --> 00:04:59.494
So, for example, the blade was taken away;

00:04:59.494 --> 00:05:01.329
we replaced it with a stick,

00:05:01.329 --> 00:05:03.414
and the stick represents the blade.

00:05:03.414 --> 00:05:06.292
And so that's why we always
train with the stick first.

00:05:06.292 --> 00:05:09.128
We also hid the movements of
the fighting arts within dance.

00:05:14.425 --> 00:05:17.386
OK, so right here, where
Liam Neeson actually

00:05:17.386 --> 00:05:19.806
takes the stick off
of his opponent,

00:05:19.806 --> 00:05:22.767
where he disarms
and uses it,

00:05:22.767 --> 00:05:25.728
you wouldn't want to 
step into that weapon

00:05:25.728 --> 00:05:28.272
or close the distance
unless you were absolutely

00:05:28.272 --> 00:05:29.982
being offensive
on the way in.

00:05:29.982 --> 00:05:32.568
When you're thinking about
going against opponents

00:05:32.568 --> 00:05:34.445
with a weapon or
attackers with a weapon,

00:05:34.445 --> 00:05:37.448
you would never want to turn
your back to that person.

00:05:37.448 --> 00:05:41.035
The right way would be
to come into the attacker

00:05:41.035 --> 00:05:44.205
being offensive, you know,
with an attack to the face,

00:05:44.205 --> 00:05:45.748
an elbow to the face.

00:05:45.748 --> 00:05:49.460
Anything that is coming
in to do some damage

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:52.255
in order to get close to that
weapon and then disarm it.

00:05:54.507 --> 00:05:58.511
Clearly, Liam has also trained
in a lot of martial arts,

00:05:58.511 --> 00:06:00.054
in Kali and other
martial arts,

00:06:00.054 --> 00:06:01.931
because you can see
it in his movements.

00:06:01.931 --> 00:06:04.350
We would give him a seven out of 10,

00:06:04.350 --> 00:06:05.977
but taking away some of the points

00:06:05.977 --> 00:06:08.688
because of the technical
errors that we noticed.

00:06:12.942 --> 00:06:15.820
The knife is coming,
and Jason is responding.

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:18.906
He's parrying here. He is
also maintaining control

00:06:18.906 --> 00:06:20.658
by staying close
to the attacker.

00:06:20.658 --> 00:06:23.202
So, in a close-quarters situation,

00:06:23.202 --> 00:06:25.621
every movement, every second counts.

00:06:25.621 --> 00:06:28.166
And as you can see from
Jason Bourne's movement,

00:06:28.166 --> 00:06:30.209
every touch is offensive.

00:06:30.209 --> 00:06:33.004
And that's very much in the Kali style.

00:06:33.004 --> 00:06:35.840
This is that famous scene
where he uses a pen.

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:37.675
I mean, it's an
everyday object

00:06:37.675 --> 00:06:39.552
that is really
quite dangerous

00:06:39.552 --> 00:06:42.013
and can be utilized
just like a knife.

00:06:42.013 --> 00:06:44.056
A key can be
used, a shoe,

00:06:44.056 --> 00:06:46.309
a magazine rolled up,
a newspaper.

00:06:46.309 --> 00:06:47.977
These can be very
powerful weapons.

00:06:51.063 --> 00:06:53.107
He was trying really
hard to kind of

00:06:53.107 --> 00:06:55.359
get that knife out
of that situation.

00:06:55.359 --> 00:06:57.320
For us in Kali, we
would just break the arm

00:06:57.320 --> 00:06:59.989
or we'd step around to the
outside a little bit more.

00:07:02.950 --> 00:07:06.454
Right there, we see just a
quick movie-camera mistake

00:07:06.454 --> 00:07:08.289
or camera-cheating
thing here.

00:07:08.289 --> 00:07:11.709
The opponent actually
throws a left hook,

00:07:11.709 --> 00:07:14.420
but Jason suddenly
catches his right arm

00:07:14.420 --> 00:07:17.340
and tries to
disarm that.

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:19.133
So, for "The Bourne Identity,"

00:07:19.133 --> 00:07:20.843
because of its great fight scene,

00:07:20.843 --> 00:07:24.347
we would probably give this
score a nine out of 10.

00:07:24.347 --> 00:07:26.891
Only docking a point
for a mistake, actually,

00:07:26.891 --> 00:07:28.184
that we noticed here.

00:07:31.562 --> 00:07:34.857
You see that the woman
fighting is getting cut, Ilsa,

00:07:34.857 --> 00:07:36.234
and then the man
is also getting cut,

00:07:36.234 --> 00:07:37.902
but yet they still
continue to fight

00:07:37.902 --> 00:07:39.904
even though they're
getting kind of slashed

00:07:39.904 --> 00:07:42.573
in different parts of
their arm, their back.

00:07:45.493 --> 00:07:47.161
Some of the knife attacks
that are coming in

00:07:47.161 --> 00:07:50.248
seem a bit wasted. A trained knife fighter

00:07:50.248 --> 00:07:52.041
or someone who's trained in the arts

00:07:52.041 --> 00:07:55.962
would be more aware to attack
more of the vital zones.

00:07:55.962 --> 00:07:59.674
So, you know, cutting the
neck or cutting the arteries,

00:07:59.674 --> 00:08:01.300
going for some of the kill shots

00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:04.011
rather than just, you know,
cutting in random spots

00:08:04.011 --> 00:08:06.514
that won't stop the
attacker from attacking.

00:08:06.514 --> 00:08:09.809
Our goal would be to move
on the outside of the body

00:08:09.809 --> 00:08:13.521
in order to capture the knife hand

00:08:13.521 --> 00:08:15.815
or kind of get around the knife hand

00:08:15.815 --> 00:08:17.942
and go for that back-of-the-neck shot.

00:08:17.942 --> 00:08:19.652
So this, the area around the neck

00:08:19.652 --> 00:08:20.861
or this whole neck area

00:08:20.861 --> 00:08:23.281
is very vulnerable as a kill zone.

00:08:23.281 --> 00:08:24.657
That's why we teach, for example,

00:08:24.657 --> 00:08:26.909
to have your hands up, protect your neck,

00:08:26.909 --> 00:08:29.412
you know, keep your eyes
and your hands moving

00:08:29.412 --> 00:08:32.623
and keep them available, so
that if there are attacks

00:08:32.623 --> 00:08:35.418
and you're able to kind of move around,

00:08:35.418 --> 00:08:37.962
you have elbows, you
have things to utilize

00:08:37.962 --> 00:08:40.214
that are your own body as weapons.

00:08:40.214 --> 00:08:43.175
But the technique that's
being demonstrated here,

00:08:43.175 --> 00:08:44.218
not so much.

00:08:49.056 --> 00:08:51.100
There's two moments
that happen here

00:08:51.100 --> 00:08:54.228
where Ilsa actually
climbs up on her attacker.

00:08:54.228 --> 00:08:57.982
So, there's a moment
where she steps on his leg

00:08:57.982 --> 00:09:00.735
in order to cut him
on the other side,

00:09:00.735 --> 00:09:02.737
and then does it again

00:09:02.737 --> 00:09:05.156
in a more exaggerated
form for her end kill.

00:09:05.156 --> 00:09:09.285
And then, again, climbs
all the way up, here we go,

00:09:09.285 --> 00:09:11.620
using the leg,
lifting yourself up,

00:09:11.620 --> 00:09:14.248
there seems to be
a lot of time here,

00:09:14.248 --> 00:09:18.002
and the person is holding
a blade and not using it.

00:09:18.002 --> 00:09:21.255
There were a few
shots where technically

00:09:21.255 --> 00:09:24.216
that person should
have been dead already,

00:09:24.216 --> 00:09:27.136
because I saw a knife
shot to the neck

00:09:27.136 --> 00:09:29.388
and then suddenly, you know,
they're able to back up,

00:09:29.388 --> 00:09:30.639
create space, and
come in again.

00:09:30.639 --> 00:09:34.185
We would probably give this
a score of six out of 10

00:09:34.185 --> 00:09:36.145
just because it's exciting to see

00:09:36.145 --> 00:09:38.314
a woman fighting so powerfully,

00:09:38.314 --> 00:09:40.816
but at the same time, just
doesn't seem so realistic.

00:09:45.154 --> 00:09:47.156
This scene is great.

00:09:47.156 --> 00:09:49.617
You know, you can see a
lot of technique here.

00:09:49.617 --> 00:09:51.994
There's a lot of
resourcefulness being shown.

00:09:51.994 --> 00:09:54.288
Clearly a lot of
Kali training.

00:09:54.288 --> 00:09:57.458
And we see a lot of
similarities here in the film

00:09:57.458 --> 00:09:59.168
to the training
that we teach.

00:10:01.462 --> 00:10:04.507
You know, he picks up a very small

00:10:04.507 --> 00:10:07.385
kind of, like, personal-hygiene item.

00:10:07.385 --> 00:10:10.763
This can be very deadly when
used in the correct way,

00:10:10.763 --> 00:10:12.640
especially with that
knife grip that he used.

00:10:12.640 --> 00:10:15.393
And so Craig actually used
a knife grip just like this,

00:10:15.393 --> 00:10:17.770
like an ice pick, and is, you know,

00:10:17.770 --> 00:10:22.024
jabbing and stabbing his
opponent with that weapon.

00:10:22.024 --> 00:10:23.025
Very effective.

00:10:25.778 --> 00:10:28.489
I think that's another
thing that distincts

00:10:28.489 --> 00:10:30.699
Filipino fighting arts
from other fighting arts,

00:10:30.699 --> 00:10:33.035
is the footwork and
is the stance work.

00:10:33.035 --> 00:10:35.496
You know, a lot of fighting
arts are actually very linear,

00:10:35.496 --> 00:10:37.289
maybe something like karate, for example.

00:10:37.289 --> 00:10:39.750
You're moving kind of in this position

00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:42.211
of linear movement,

00:10:42.211 --> 00:10:44.088
but in the Filipino fighting style,

00:10:44.088 --> 00:10:45.589
our movement is always based

00:10:45.589 --> 00:10:48.759
on the triangle or
triangular kind of motion.

00:10:48.759 --> 00:10:50.469
And so our footwork would reflect that

00:10:50.469 --> 00:10:52.847
in sidestepping, you know, to the side

00:10:52.847 --> 00:10:55.850
or stepping 45 degrees, which
Craig really demonstrates.

00:10:55.850 --> 00:10:59.270
For the effectivity of the
technique and the movements,

00:10:59.270 --> 00:11:01.647
"Quantum of Solace" would
get an eight out of 10.

00:11:06.235 --> 00:11:07.987
This is a
fantastic scene.

00:11:07.987 --> 00:11:12.867
First and foremost, it is
great because it gives credit

00:11:12.867 --> 00:11:16.078
to the Filipino culture
where this knife came from.

00:11:16.078 --> 00:11:19.748
So, the balisong is another
name for the butterfly knife,

00:11:19.748 --> 00:11:21.917
which originated here in the Philippines.

00:11:21.917 --> 00:11:25.421
It actually came from a region
in Luzon called Batangas,

00:11:25.421 --> 00:11:27.047
and it is a butterfly knife

00:11:27.047 --> 00:11:29.925
because of the way that it
opens up like a butterfly

00:11:29.925 --> 00:11:33.053
and has double-edged blades on each side.

00:11:33.053 --> 00:11:34.763
So it's very, very dangerous.

00:11:37.641 --> 00:11:38.559
Mindy: Wow.

00:11:38.559 --> 00:11:41.187
Jamie: That's probably
something that you first learn

00:11:41.187 --> 00:11:43.355
when you learn balisong, is
how to open and close it.

00:11:43.355 --> 00:11:46.734
And so it's very, very,
you know, exciting and fun.

00:11:46.734 --> 00:11:49.195
There is moments of where
she's using the blade

00:11:49.195 --> 00:11:53.115
to actually tap or hit on the
body that is quite realistic.

00:11:53.115 --> 00:11:56.494
Damon: Smaller-caliber
version of what design?

00:11:56.494 --> 00:11:58.287
Mindy: Uh, Eugene Stoner's AR-10.

00:11:58.287 --> 00:11:59.914
Jamie: Kali is for
everyone.

00:11:59.914 --> 00:12:03.709
It's especially useful
in situations of survival

00:12:03.709 --> 00:12:06.837
because of the way that
we are able to respond

00:12:06.837 --> 00:12:09.507
to a weapons attack even without a weapon.

00:12:09.507 --> 00:12:12.843
It's particularly useful
for women and children

00:12:12.843 --> 00:12:17.223
or smaller folks to protect
against a larger attacker.

00:12:17.223 --> 00:12:20.559
Our family system is
called Pekiti-Tirsia Kali,

00:12:20.559 --> 00:12:22.978
and it originates from the Western Visayas

00:12:22.978 --> 00:12:24.271
of the Philippines.

00:12:24.271 --> 00:12:26.732
And this blade culture has
been protecting the family

00:12:26.732 --> 00:12:29.902
since 1898 and even traces its roots

00:12:29.902 --> 00:12:32.029
to more than 2,000 years ago.

00:12:32.029 --> 00:12:35.199
We would definitely give
this a nine out of 10,

00:12:35.199 --> 00:12:38.160
just because we love the
technique that Chloë is doing,

00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:40.538
but we would like to see
a little more offensive

00:12:40.538 --> 00:12:42.206
with the knife itself.

00:12:44.333 --> 00:12:46.335
[screaming]

00:12:46.335 --> 00:12:48.879
We have someone coming in
with the straight grip,

00:12:48.879 --> 00:12:50.756
and we have someone
coming in with

00:12:50.756 --> 00:12:52.508
what looks like
a karambit,

00:12:52.508 --> 00:12:55.261
which is coming on the
backside of that blade.

00:13:00.975 --> 00:13:03.269
When you get real, real
close to the weapon,

00:13:03.269 --> 00:13:05.521
the weapon then
becomes rendered useless,

00:13:05.521 --> 00:13:07.064
which is the point
sometimes, right?

00:13:07.064 --> 00:13:10.651
Like, sometimes you need to
step into that weapon hand

00:13:10.651 --> 00:13:12.570
or to get close offensively

00:13:12.570 --> 00:13:15.030
in order to take
control of that weapon,

00:13:15.030 --> 00:13:17.449
or you need to find a way to distract

00:13:17.449 --> 00:13:19.702
and knock the senses in other ways

00:13:19.702 --> 00:13:21.245
in order to get out of that situation.

00:13:21.245 --> 00:13:25.583
So very intense close-quarters
situation with blades,

00:13:25.583 --> 00:13:30.462
but also very, yeah, I would
just say very interesting.

00:13:30.462 --> 00:13:32.464
You know, when it comes
to strength and size,

00:13:32.464 --> 00:13:35.217
it doesn't really matter
when it comes to the blade.

00:13:35.217 --> 00:13:37.720
So, you know, the blade
is the equalizer here.

00:13:37.720 --> 00:13:40.973
Because the other person
fighting may be smaller

00:13:40.973 --> 00:13:43.309
or, you know, not as
strong, it doesn't mean that

00:13:43.309 --> 00:13:46.478
they're not as effective
with the use of a knife.

00:13:50.524 --> 00:13:51.859
You can always
throw elbows

00:13:51.859 --> 00:13:53.569
and use the elbow
as a weapon.

00:13:53.569 --> 00:13:55.863
So, he's grabbing
and kneeing

00:13:55.863 --> 00:13:57.531
and hitting over
the head

00:13:57.531 --> 00:13:59.325
when he could easily
just cut that hand

00:13:59.325 --> 00:14:02.161
that he already has in
his hand, in his power.

00:14:02.161 --> 00:14:04.163
So that was technically a moment

00:14:04.163 --> 00:14:07.207
where we would say isn't very realistic

00:14:07.207 --> 00:14:09.376
and is a little more
exaggerated for camera.

00:14:09.376 --> 00:14:11.920
There's, like, that big, open swing,

00:14:11.920 --> 00:14:14.840
and they're extending the
fight where they don't need to.

00:14:17.384 --> 00:14:19.136
So, usually in movies
you'll see, like,

00:14:19.136 --> 00:14:20.888
total destruction
and chaos

00:14:20.888 --> 00:14:24.224
and collateral damage
and collateral murder.

00:14:24.224 --> 00:14:26.101
But in this case, you know,
they kind of stop and wait

00:14:26.101 --> 00:14:27.936
for everyone to pass, and
they get right back into it,

00:14:27.936 --> 00:14:29.563
so it's kind of funny.

00:14:29.563 --> 00:14:32.483
Probably give it about a seven out of 10,

00:14:32.483 --> 00:14:35.194
because they did great
with the knife grips,

00:14:35.194 --> 00:14:37.279
but technically speaking,
there were a few errors

00:14:37.279 --> 00:14:39.198
that we noticed that kept the fight scene

00:14:39.198 --> 00:14:41.075
a little too long than it had to be.

00:14:45.746 --> 00:14:49.583
So, right away, we're
seeing a very familiar

00:14:49.583 --> 00:14:52.670
Filipino martial arts
double-stick drill,

00:14:52.670 --> 00:14:55.172
one of the first things
or one of the early things

00:14:55.172 --> 00:14:57.299
that you would learn when you're learning

00:14:57.299 --> 00:15:00.052
how to utilize a double stick

00:15:00.052 --> 00:15:01.887
or double sticks as a weapon.

00:15:01.887 --> 00:15:03.430
So this is a great drill.

00:15:03.430 --> 00:15:07.017
We often call this "upper
six" or "heaven six."

00:15:07.017 --> 00:15:10.979
The actors are not properly
chambering their weapons.

00:15:10.979 --> 00:15:13.774
So that's something that
wouldn't be seen

00:15:13.774 --> 00:15:16.819
in a real Filipino
martial arts training.

00:15:16.819 --> 00:15:17.778
When you have the sticks,

00:15:17.778 --> 00:15:20.030
every time you strike, you chamber.

00:15:20.030 --> 00:15:24.243
So the idea is that you're
always bringing the stick back

00:15:24.243 --> 00:15:26.203
before hitting again.

00:15:27.788 --> 00:15:31.625
It is a bit of a camera-cheating
angle that's happening.

00:15:31.625 --> 00:15:35.087
So, the way that we do
this particular drill

00:15:35.087 --> 00:15:36.422
is with a lot of footwork.

00:15:36.422 --> 00:15:38.799
You'll see us moving
with the double sticks

00:15:38.799 --> 00:15:42.803
and moving forward and
back, exchanging feet,

00:15:42.803 --> 00:15:46.390
and moving around in a
circle, most importantly.

00:15:46.390 --> 00:15:47.975
At first glance you might think,

00:15:47.975 --> 00:15:50.102
"Oh, well, they are moving
around in a circle,"

00:15:50.102 --> 00:15:52.438
until you look carefully and realize

00:15:52.438 --> 00:15:54.940
that the actors are actually
not doing the footwork

00:15:54.940 --> 00:15:56.316
and it's the camera itself

00:15:56.316 --> 00:15:58.318
that's moving around in a circle.

00:16:01.196 --> 00:16:02.614
They do the drill,

00:16:02.614 --> 00:16:05.409
and one of the guys
hits the other one.

00:16:05.409 --> 00:16:08.036
If you're doing the drill
correctly, you wouldn't get hit,

00:16:08.036 --> 00:16:10.372
because you would
continuously be doing the drill.

00:16:10.372 --> 00:16:11.957
So that's another
thing that we noticed.

00:16:11.957 --> 00:16:14.668
Like, "Oh, you know, that
doesn't seem too realistic,

00:16:14.668 --> 00:16:17.254
because they stopped."
Why did they stop?

00:16:17.254 --> 00:16:20.591
We would probably give it a
four out of 10 for realism.

00:16:23.802 --> 00:16:25.721
I think it's one of
the greatest movies,

00:16:25.721 --> 00:16:29.475
and it definitely demonstrates
a ton of Kali work.

00:16:29.475 --> 00:16:33.604
I mean, Denzel here is using a long blade,

00:16:33.604 --> 00:16:35.481
he's fighting against multiple opponents,

00:16:35.481 --> 00:16:37.900
he's using all kinds of slashes,

00:16:37.900 --> 00:16:42.362
circular movements, lateral
strikes, he's thrusting.

00:16:45.741 --> 00:16:48.285
He even puts the blade
down at one point

00:16:48.285 --> 00:16:49.828
and then brings
it back out.

00:16:49.828 --> 00:16:54.583
So this is really a
great demonstration

00:16:54.583 --> 00:16:57.002
of extensive
Kali training.

00:16:57.002 --> 00:17:00.631
I do know that Denzel
did train with Jeff Imada

00:17:00.631 --> 00:17:03.467
at the Dan Inosanto
Academy in Los Angeles

00:17:03.467 --> 00:17:04.635
to develop these skills.

00:17:04.635 --> 00:17:07.638
Jeff Imada does train
at the Inosanto Academy,

00:17:07.638 --> 00:17:09.389
where we've trained
with our grandmaster.

00:17:09.389 --> 00:17:10.974
We've had the pleasure
of meeting Jeff.

00:17:10.974 --> 00:17:14.019
We're talking great classic
choreographers here.

00:17:18.232 --> 00:17:21.693
He is countering every single
attack that is coming his way.

00:17:21.693 --> 00:17:25.072
That's the mindset of Kali,
that instead of, you know,

00:17:25.072 --> 00:17:28.534
moving around and trying
to go after each person,

00:17:28.534 --> 00:17:31.203
if you stay in one place and
wait for the attack to come

00:17:31.203 --> 00:17:32.788
but be able to move the feet

00:17:32.788 --> 00:17:34.748
and the blade in an effective way,

00:17:34.748 --> 00:17:36.875
then you're able to
respond very effectively.

00:17:36.875 --> 00:17:40.796
This is a great example of
not having to do too much

00:17:40.796 --> 00:17:44.258
but also doing a lot in that one space

00:17:44.258 --> 00:17:45.425
that you're confined in.

00:17:45.425 --> 00:17:48.178
This weapon looks
like a machete.

00:17:48.178 --> 00:17:49.513
A machete here
in the Philippines

00:17:49.513 --> 00:17:50.722
would be
called a bolo.

00:17:50.722 --> 00:17:53.475
You know, it would be
considered an everyday blade,

00:17:53.475 --> 00:17:56.937
something that would normally be used to

00:17:56.937 --> 00:18:00.899
cut sugarcane or to engage in farming

00:18:00.899 --> 00:18:02.943
or fishing or building your house.

00:18:02.943 --> 00:18:06.321
So it seems like just an
everyday kind of blade,

00:18:06.321 --> 00:18:08.490
but the way that -- and the length of it.

00:18:08.490 --> 00:18:10.826
So, the length of it is quite long.

00:18:10.826 --> 00:18:13.328
It's considered a long-range weapon.

00:18:13.328 --> 00:18:15.581
And because of that, it
can be very effective

00:18:15.581 --> 00:18:17.165
with fighting against multiple opponents.

00:18:17.165 --> 00:18:19.668
And there's even a scene in
the beginning of the movie

00:18:19.668 --> 00:18:22.379
where he's on a
highway patrol

00:18:22.379 --> 00:18:25.549
and someone
tries to attack,

00:18:25.549 --> 00:18:28.635
and he kind of just
slices the hand off

00:18:28.635 --> 00:18:30.929
in a swift, single motion.

00:18:30.929 --> 00:18:32.556
That is the kind of movement

00:18:32.556 --> 00:18:34.766
that we are trained to also develop.

00:18:34.766 --> 00:18:36.602
We have to give it a 10 out of 10.

00:18:36.602 --> 00:18:38.854
Excellent technique, great execution.

00:18:43.901 --> 00:18:46.278
Gotta love Lucy Liu.

00:18:46.278 --> 00:18:49.156
You know, she comes
storming in with power,

00:18:49.156 --> 00:18:50.324
with confidence,

00:18:50.324 --> 00:18:53.660
and she's got not one
but two weapons.

00:18:53.660 --> 00:18:56.496
So you're seeing a lot of
exciting movement here.

00:18:58.790 --> 00:19:01.960
She's attacking different
levels, high, low,

00:19:01.960 --> 00:19:04.546
you know, arms,
heads, feet.

00:19:04.546 --> 00:19:06.924
It's just such a
great scene

00:19:06.924 --> 00:19:09.635
and an example of
how a double weapon

00:19:09.635 --> 00:19:11.553
can be used against
multiple opponents,

00:19:11.553 --> 00:19:13.388
especially in
the distance

00:19:13.388 --> 00:19:15.223
that she's maintaining
with them.

00:19:16.600 --> 00:19:18.560
The only thing
about this film

00:19:18.560 --> 00:19:21.980
is the interesting 
component

00:19:21.980 --> 00:19:24.775
that every single one
of these people

00:19:24.775 --> 00:19:27.235
are fully padded
in a ton of gear.

00:19:27.235 --> 00:19:29.196
We would doubt
that those batons

00:19:29.196 --> 00:19:32.491
would do that much damage
to these trained men

00:19:32.491 --> 00:19:35.410
that are all padded up
with that kind of gear.

00:19:35.410 --> 00:19:37.829
And also the fact
that everyone here

00:19:37.829 --> 00:19:41.083
that she's fighting
against has a major weapon,

00:19:41.083 --> 00:19:43.251
they have a gun, and
they're not using it.

00:19:43.251 --> 00:19:44.962
They're just kind of
being thrown on the floor.

00:19:44.962 --> 00:19:46.129
No one's
picking it up.

00:19:49.007 --> 00:19:51.426
She's utilizing both
of the weapons,

00:19:51.426 --> 00:19:52.678
she's extending
her arms,

00:19:52.678 --> 00:19:54.763
and she's moving
in full circles.

00:19:54.763 --> 00:19:55.847
You know, that's
very effective.

00:19:55.847 --> 00:19:57.808
That's the kind of
stuff that we teach.

00:19:57.808 --> 00:20:01.353
To be able to maneuver from
one opponent to the other,

00:20:01.353 --> 00:20:03.146
hitting folks at the same time.

00:20:03.146 --> 00:20:06.775
She has good motion, range
of motion with her weapons.

00:20:06.775 --> 00:20:10.195
She chambers them, brings them
back to the body, hits again.

00:20:10.195 --> 00:20:13.490
You know, multiple attacks
here against that last person.

00:20:13.490 --> 00:20:15.242
You know, a lot of circular movements.

00:20:15.242 --> 00:20:17.619
The realism of this scenario,
we'd actually give this

00:20:17.619 --> 00:20:19.579
probably a six out of 10,

00:20:19.579 --> 00:20:22.082
just because we think
Lucy was doing great work,

00:20:22.082 --> 00:20:24.918
just doesn't seem so realistic
compared to her opponents.

